# Reference material — deliberately NOT tracked.
#
# reference/ holds Nels Anderson's EGA Trek (1988-92) shareware package and
# material derived from it. Its licence (reference/manual.txt, "SHAREWARE")
# requires that any distributed copy include egatrek.exe, egatrek.doc,
# egatrek.ref, orderfrm.txt, egatrek.txt and file_id.diz *unmodified*. What is
# on disk here is neither complete (no orderfrm.txt, no file_id.diz) nor
# unmodified (manual.txt is egatrek.doc with control codes stripped;
# EGATREK_unpacked.exe is LZEXE-unpacked; strings.txt is extracted). Publishing
# it would be redistribution on terms the licence does not grant.
#
# Fetch it yourself from the Internet Archive item `EGATrek`.
reference/

# Generated note data. Build output rather than a licence matter now: the music
# is composed in tools/make_music.py and is this port's own, so it CAN ship.
# (tools/gen_music.py, which extracts Anderson's note data instead, still
# exists and its output still must not be committed or released.)
c128/src/music_data.h
c128/src/music_data.c

# AND THE MEGA65'S COPIES, which were TRACKED until 2026-09-05 while the C128's
# were not. That asymmetry was the hazard: `make -C mega65 music` copies
# whatever is in c128/src/ across, so the extractor's output would have landed
# in a path git was willing to commit, sidestepping the rule above. Both ports
# regenerate these from tools/make_music.py -- verified by deleting them and
# building -- so tracking them bought nothing.
mega65/src/music_data.h
mega65/src/music_data.c
x16/src/music_data.h
x16/src/music_data.c
amiga/src/music_data.h
amiga/src/music_data.c
atari/src/music_data.h
atari/src/music_data.c
falcon/src/music_data.h
falcon/src/music_data.c
coco3/src/music_data.h
coco3/src/music_data.c

# cmoc's intermediate 6809 assembly. It writes <basename>.s beside its output
# and removes it on success, so a run from the repository ROOT clobbers
# anything there with the same name -- which is how trek.s, planet.s, hof.s
# and serial.s came to be COMMITTED by the CoCo 3 scoping run (8f066e7) and
# then deleted by the next one. coco3/Makefile passes --intdir=build so they
# never land here again; these patterns are the belt to that braces.
/trek.s
/planet.s
/hof.s
/serial.s
/shift.s

# Build output
build/

# BUT NOT coco3/hardware/ -- disks written for a real machine live there
# precisely because `make release-clean` wipes every build/. The .dsk itself
# is still a build product and stays out of git; the directory and its README
# are tracked so the path is not something somebody has to rediscover.
coco3/hardware/*.dsk
*.o
*.prg
*.xex
*.d64
*.adf

.DS_Store

# Python bytecode from tools/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
x16/build/

# MAME writes these into whatever directory it is run from -- the IIgs rig runs
# from iigs/, so its own config and battery RAM land there. Emulator state, not
# port source: a committed nvram/rtc makes every run start from one machine's
# clock and a committed cfg pins the input map of whoever ran it last.
iigs/cfg/
iigs/nvram/

# The F256K port's build tree, like every other port's -- AND MAME'S OWN STATE.
# Running MAME from this directory drops cfg/ and nvram/ here; they are the
# emulator's, not the port's, and they were staged for a commit once.
f256k/build/
f256k/cfg/
f256k/nvram/

# AND THE GIME PORT'S, WHICH WERE COMMITTED. coco3gime/cfg/{coco3,default}.cfg
# and coco3gime/snap/coco3/0000.png were in the tree of a RELEASED port, from
# before the rule above existed -- three files of one machine's input map and
# one screenshot nothing referenced. Removed 2026-09-18 by a sweep.
coco3gime/cfg/
coco3gime/nvram/
coco3gime/snap/
